{"subjectId":"data-science","id":"pandas-basics","title":"Series & DataFrames","group":"Pandas","order":5,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Pandas gives Python two labelled data structures — the one-dimensional <code>Series</code> and the two-dimensional <code>DataFrame</code> — the everyday tools for tabular data.</p>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list\"></i>Series</h3><p>One column: values plus an <strong>index</strong> (labels). Like a NumPy array with names.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-table\"></i>DataFrame</h3><p>A table: several Series sharing one index — rows and named columns, like a spreadsheet or SQL table.</p></div></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list\"></i>Series</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\ns = pd.Series(np.array([\"g\", \"e\", \"e\", \"k\", \"s\"]))\nprint(s.tolist(), s.index.tolist())      # ['g', 'e', 'e', 'k', 's'] [0, 1, 2, 3, 4]\nprices = pd.Series([40, 25, 60], index=[\"tea\", \"milk\", \"coffee\"])\nprint(prices[\"coffee\"], prices.max())    # 60 60\nprint(prices[prices &gt; 30].to_dict())     # {'tea': 40, 'coffee': 60}</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-table-plus\"></i>Creating DataFrames</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import pandas as pd\nfrom_rows = pd.DataFrame([[\"a\", 10], [\"b\", 15], [\"c\", 14]], columns=[\"Name\", \"Age\"])\nfrom_dict = pd.DataFrame({\"Name\": [\"Tom\", \"Jack\", \"Steve\", \"Ricky\"], \"Age\": [28, 34, 29, 42]})\nprint(from_rows.shape, from_dict.shape)          # (3, 2) (4, 2)\nprint(from_dict)</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>(3, 2) (4, 2)\n    Name  Age\n0    Tom   28\n1   Jack   34\n2  Steve   29\n3  Ricky   42</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Pitfall</div><p>Building a DataFrame from a NumPy array of mixed values (as the notebook does with <code>np.array([['a', 10], …])</code>) turns <strong>every</strong> value into a string — <code>Age</code> would be text. Use lists or a dict so each column keeps its own type.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nbad = pd.DataFrame(np.array([[\"a\", 10], [\"b\", 15]]), columns=[\"Name\", \"Age\"])\ngood = pd.DataFrame([[\"a\", 10], [\"b\", 15]], columns=[\"Name\", \"Age\"])\nprint(bad[\"Age\"].tolist(), good[\"Age\"].tolist())    # ['10', '15'] [10, 15]</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-click\"></i>Selecting columns and rows</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import pandas as pd\nfrom io import StringIO\n\ncsv = \"\"\"species,size,fragrance,height_cm\nrose,medium,mild,48.55\nshoeblack plant,medium,mild,147.07\nshoeblack plant,medium,none,102.93\nhibiscus,large,none,184.00\nshoeblack plant,large,mild,83.07\nrose,small,strong,35.20\nhibiscus,large,,126.69\nrose,medium,mild,88.11\n\"\"\"\ndf = pd.read_csv(StringIO(csv))       # same call you'd use with a file path\n\nprint(df.columns.tolist())             # ['species', 'size', 'fragrance', 'height_cm']\nprint(type(df[\"species\"]).__name__)    # Series\nprint(df[[\"species\", \"fragrance\"]].shape)   # (8, 2)\nprint(df.loc[3, \"species\"])            # hibiscus      ← by label\nprint(df.iloc[0][\"species\"], df.iloc[0][\"height_cm\"])   # rose 48.55     ← by position</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Syntax</th><th>Selects</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>df[\"col\"]</code></td><td>One column → Series</td></tr><tr><td><code>df[[\"a\", \"b\"]]</code></td><td>Several columns → DataFrame</td></tr><tr><td><code>df.loc[row_label, col_label]</code></td><td>By labels (slices include the end)</td></tr><tr><td><code>df.iloc[i, j]</code></td><td>By integer position (slices exclude the end)</td></tr><tr><td><code>df[df[\"x\"] &gt; 5]</code></td><td>Rows matching a condition</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-file-spreadsheet\"></i>Reading and writing files</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import pandas as pd\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\"Names\": [\"Geeks\", \"For\", \"Geeks\"]}, index=[\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"])\ndf.to_csv(\"names.csv\")                         # writes the index too\nback = pd.read_csv(\"names.csv\", index_col=0)   # read it back, using column 0 as the index\nprint(back.equals(df))                         # True\ndf.to_csv(\"names_no_index.csv\", index=False)   # usually what you want for exports</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>read_csv</code> has many options: <code>sep</code>, <code>header</code>, <code>index_col</code>, <code>usecols</code> (pick columns), <code>nrows</code> (read only the first n rows), <code>skiprows</code>, <code>parse_dates</code>. There's also <code>read_excel</code>, <code>read_json</code>, <code>read_sql</code> and <code>to_…</code> counterparts.</p></div></section>"}